|~ `::frame`, the kind of one level of the trace a failure leaves, and
   what is declared under it: the parts its kind wires, the refusal of a
   payload that holds no subject and steps, and the verbs that read its
   steps.
   The rules it holds: [QSERR#trace]. ~|

:frame
  |~~ A frame is one level of the trace a failure leaves, the step that
      failed there with the value it met: the subject of the step, the
      steps of its level from the one that failed, and, where the step ran
      its code on a part of its subject, the path of that part.
      `::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)]` is `add 1`, which failed on `"x"`,
      and `mul 2`, which did not run; `::frame[[1 "x" 3] ~(map (mul 2)) /1]`
      is `map`, which failed where its code ran on the element at `/1`.

      A trace holds a frame for each step that raised a failure or handed
      it on, a frame a line, and a frame prints on one line whatever its
      subject holds. Its kind names its parts, `subject`, `unfinished` and
      `path`, each read by its name, `!| trace | last | path`, so
      `!| trace | last | subject | get /1` reads the element the code of
      `map` failed on; `failed` and `skipped` read the step that failed
      and the steps it skipped. The steps skipped at the level that reads
      a failure run again on another value by `apply`.
      ::snippet~([1 "x" 3] | map (mul 2) !| trace | last)
      ::link~(::trace) ::link~(::qlang/errors)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ The subject, the steps and the path. ~~| ::vec
    | wires :subject
        |~~ The value the step that failed there met, `"x"` for
            `"x" | add 1`: the first frame of a trace holds the subject the
            step that failed met, and the frame of a verb that ran its code
            on a part of its subject the whole subject, its `path` naming
            the part.
        ~~|
        /0
    | wires :unfinished
        |~~ The steps of the level of the frame from the one that failed, a
            quote: the step that failed and the steps the failure skipped
            after it, `::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)] | unfinished`
            answering `~(add 1 | mul 2)`, of which `failed` answers the
            first and `skipped` the rest.
        ~~|
        /1 ::quote
    | wires :path
        |~~ The path of the part of the subject the step failed at: null
            where it failed at no part:
            `[1 "x" 3] | map (mul 2) !| trace | last | path` answers `/1`.
            Each part is named by the path of its index or of its key. A
            verb names the part its code failed on: `map`, `filter`,
            `every`, `any` and `reduce` and the keys of `sort`, `min`,
            `max`, `groupBy` and `indexBy`. It names the part whose answer
            it refused, the verdict of a predicate and the key of a
            partition. It names the part it refused itself, an element of
            `sum`, `avg`, `zip`, `join`, `toPath`, `toMap` and a fold of
            the algebra. `update` names the path it wrote, and a step the
            part its modifier failed at. The subject reads the part by it,
            `get (f | path)` with `f` the frame.
        ~~|
        ~(query /2) #[::null ::path]
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A frame is the vector of the subject a step met, the quote of its
            steps and the path of a part, `::frame["x" ~(add 1)]`; a vector
            under a tag gives the vector beneath by `payload`, and a set
            turns into one by `toVec`.
        ~~|
    | holds :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError
        |~~ A frame holds one step at least, the step that failed, and after
            its steps, where it holds one, the path of the part of the
            subject the step ran its code on, and nothing past it:
            `::frame["x" ~(add 1)]`, `::frame[[1 "x"] ~(map (mul 2)) /1]`.
        ~~|
        ~(allOf (get /1 | count | gt 0) (count | lte 3) (anyOf (count | eq 2) (get /2 | is ::path)))
    | proves :holdsTheStepThatFailedAndTheValueItMet
        |~~ The frame of `add 1` holds the value it met. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | first | eq ::frame["x" ~(add 1)])
    | proves :holdsThePathOfThePart
        |~~ The frame of `map` holds the path of the element its code
            failed on.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map (mul 2) !| trace | last | eq ::frame[[1 "x" 3] ~(map (mul 2)) /1])
    | proves :printsOnOneLine
        |~~ A frame prints on one line whatever its subject holds. ~~|
        ~({:user {:tags [1 2]}} | get /group !| trace | first | print | lines | count | eq 1)
    | proves :readsItsPartsByTheirNames
        |~~ The parts of a frame are read by the names its kind wires, bare. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | first | get [subject unfinished path] | eq ["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2) null])
    | proves :refusesTheIndexOfAPart
        |~~ No path reads beneath the tag of a frame, by an index nor by a
            name.
        ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | get [(get /0/0 !| tag) (get /0/subject !| repair)] | eq [::path/TagClosedError ~(get /0 | subject)])
    | proves :UnfinishedKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ The unfinished steps of a frame are a quote. ~~|
        ~(::frame[1 2] !| tag | eq ::frame/UnfinishedKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :subject/holdsTheWholeSubjectOfAVerb
        |~~ The frame of `map` holds the vector it ran its code over. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map (mul 2) !| trace | last | subject | eq [1 "x" 3])
    | proves :path/namesTheKeyOfAMap
        |~~ Over a map the path names the key. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake "six"} | map (add 1) !| trace | last | path | eq /cake)
    | proves :path/namesThePathUpdateWrote
        |~~ `update` names the path it wrote. ~~|
        ~({:page {:visits "one"}} | update /page/visits (add 1) !| trace | last | path | eq /page/visits)
    | proves :path/namesTheElementAVerbRefused
        |~~ `sum` names the element it refused. ~~|
        ~([1 "x"] | sum !| trace | first | path | eq /1)
    | proves :path/namesThePartAModifierFailedAt
        |~~ A step names the part its modifier failed at. ~~|
        ~([1 "x"] | eq sum !| trace | first | path | eq /1)
    | proves :path/answersNullWhereTheStepFailedAtNoPart
        |~~ A step that failed at no part names none. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | first | path | eq null)
    | proves :path/readsThePartFromTheSubject
        |~~ The subject of the frame reads the part by its path. ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map (mul 2) !| trace | last | with {:at path} (subject | get at) | eq "x")
    | proves :UnfinishedNotGivenError/refusesASubjectAlone
        |~~ A subject with no steps is no frame. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x"] !| tag | eq ::frame/UnfinishedNotGivenError)
    | proves :PathKindNotTakenError/refusesAThirdPartThatIsNoPath
        |~~ A third part that is no path is refused. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~(add 1) 1] !| tag | eq ::frame/PathKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError/refusesNoStep
        |~~ The steps of a frame hold the step that failed. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~()] !| tag | eq ::frame/PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError)
    | proves :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError/refusesANullPath
        |~~ A frame of a step that failed at no part writes no third part. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~(add 1) null] !| tag | eq ::frame/PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError)
    | proves :PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError/refusesAPartPastThePath
        |~~ Nothing stands past the path. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~(add 1) /0 5] !| tag | eq ::frame/PayloadNotSubjectAndStepsError)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map is no frame. ~~|
        ~(::frame{:failed ~(add 1)} !| tag | eq ::frame/PayloadKindNotTakenError);

:frame/failed
  |~~ Answers the step that failed, the quote of its one step,
      `::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)] | failed` answering `~(add 1)`, so
      `!| trace | map failed` reads the trace of a failure a step at a
      time, from the step that refused to the step of the level that
      reads it. The step is code that runs again by `apply` and is written
      as text by `toString`, and `failed | first` reads the record of its form,
      a `::call`.
      ::link~(::frame/skipped) ::link~(::frame/unfinished)
      ::link~(::trace)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The step that failed, a quote of its one step. ~~| ::quote
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `failed` reads a frame, which the trace of a failure holds,
            `"x" | add 1 !| trace | first | failed`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheStepThatFailed
        |~~ The step that failed is the first of the steps of the frame. ~~|
        ~(::frame["x" ~(add 1 | mul 2)] | failed | eq ~(add 1))
    | proves :readsATraceAStepAtATime
        |~~ The steps of a trace read from where the failure arose outward.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "x" 3] | map (mul 2) !| trace | map failed | eq [~(mul 2) ~(map (mul 2))])
    | proves :readsTheRecordOfItsStepByFirst
        |~~ The first of the quote is the record of the step, a call. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | first | failed | first | tag | eq ::call)
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesAQuote
        |~~ A quote is no frame. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | frame/failed !| tag | eq ::frame/failed/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(unfinished | take 1);

:frame/skipped
  |~~ Answers the steps the failure skipped after the step that failed, a
      quote, the empty quote where it skipped none:
      `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped` answers `~(mul 2)`,
      the steps left at the level that reads the failure, which run again
      on another value by `apply`.
      ::link~(::frame/failed) ::link~(::frame/unfinished)
      ::link~(::any/apply)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The steps skipped, a quote. ~~| ::quote
    | throws :SubjectNotFrameError
        |~~ `skipped` reads a frame, which the trace of a failure holds,
            `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheStepsSkipped
        |~~ The steps after the one that failed did not run. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped | eq ~(mul 2))
    | proves :answersTheEmptyQuoteForNone
        |~~ A step that skipped none answers the empty quote. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| trace | last | skipped | eq ~())
    | proves :runsAgainByApply
        |~~ The steps skipped run again on another value. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trace | last | skipped | with {:rest it} (5 | apply rest) | eq 10)
    | proves :SubjectNotFrameError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector is no frame. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | skipped !| tag | eq ::frame/skipped/SubjectNotFrameError)
    | impl ~(unfinished | drop 1);
